{
  "result": "I have always loved the hour just before the library closes for the night. The lamps turn amber, the chairs scrape softly, and even the dust seems to slow down in the air. It is the only time when a building built for everyone feels like it belongs to one person. I wander between shelves without a list, letting my fingers read spines the way other people read faces. A cookbook from another country promises dinners I will probably never cook but love imagining anyway. A novel with a cracked spine suggests someone else stayed up too late turning its pages. That small evidence of strangers feels comforting, like footprints proving a trail leads somewhere worth going. Outside, buses sigh at the curb and the sky folds itself into deep blue layers. Inside, the radiators tick their quiet applause for every reader reluctant to leave a good paragraph. I think we collect these unfinished moments because finishing everything would leave us nowhere to return. A bookmark marks mercy more than progress; it says you may rest and still belong to the story. Soon the librarian will announce closing in that gentle voice reserved for waking sleepers on trains. I will choose one book almost at random, trusting chance to teach me what intention missed. Then I will step into the cold with warm pages under my arm, already anticipating tomorrow evening. Some books wait years for the right reader, patient as seeds, certain that spring eventually finds every buried thing.",
